#exception-box(title: "The Seven Fatal CUET Traps in Planning")[
  1. *Policy vs Procedure Trap:* Policy guides *managerial thinking*; Procedure specifies *chronological action steps*.
  2. *Method vs Procedure Confusion:* Method is the standardized way to do *one specific step*; Procedure is the *entire sequence* of steps.
  3. *The Rule Discretion Fallacy:* Believing managers can waive rules at will. Rules permit *zero managerial discretion* and carry penalties.
  4. *Objectives & Strategy Classification Trap:* Grouping them under Standing Plans. Objectives and Strategies are *neither standing nor single-use*.
  5. *The False Sense of Security Trap:* Assuming a previously successful plan guarantees future success. Past success does not insulate against dynamic changes.
  6. *Premises Ordering Error:* Forgetting where premises fit. Developing premises comes *immediately after setting objectives*, before finding alternatives.
  7. *Budget Category Confusion:* Treating budgets only as accounting tools. A budget is a *single-use plan* expressed numerically that acts as a controlling standard.
]

